  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nCentral idea: We will use a multi-resolution optimization strategy to find a lower Erd\u0151s minimum overlap constant C. We start with the provided 42-point parent solution and consider increasing grid resolutions by doubling (from 42 to 84, 84 to 168, etc.). At each grid size, we perform a structured optimization to find the best solution.\n\nWhy this should improve results: \n- The parent solution (C=0.38085) is likely stuck in a local minimum. Higher resolutions allow us to:\n  - Capture finer transition details between 0 and 1\n  - Better approximate the continuous optimization problem\n  - Potentially break away from suboptimal structures inherited from lower resolutions\n\nBottleneck addressed: \n- Performance via multi-resolution: We evaluate only promising solutions at each stage rather than brute-force global search\n- Constraint handling: Project solutions to satisfy integral and range constraints\n- Feasibility: Construct valid solutions explicitly by interpolation and repair\n\nThe core limitation is the trade-off between grid resolution and optimization time - higher grids use more expensive L-BFGS-B searches but may yield better results.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n- Representation: Piecewise-constant step functions approximated by grid samples\n- Variables: h[x] \u2208 [0,1] for x in [0,2] sampled at n points\n- Objective: Minimize C\u2085 = dx * max_k (np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode=\"full\"))\n  where dx = 2.0/n\n- Constraints: \n  - Integral: sum(h)*dx = 1.0\n  - Box: 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i\n\nDerivation details:\nThe objective measures maximum overlap between shifts. The correlation mode=\"full\" accounts for non-circular shifts. The integrand falls to zero outside [0,2] per problem domain specifications.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n  initialize n_list = [42, 84, 168, 336, 672] (terminate if too slow to compute dx=2.0/n)\n  best_score = float('inf')\n  best_h = None\n  For each grid size n in n_list:\n      if grid is 42:\n          h_sample = initial_h_values if provided else [1]*21 + [0]*21  (adjusted to meet sum constraint)\n          Compute dx = 2.0/42\n      else:  # Double from previous grid\n          Interpolate previous distribution to new grid points using np.interp while respecting endpoint behavior\n\n      candidate_pool = GenerateInitialCandidates(n, num_starts=10):\n          - Start from interpolated parent\n          - Add random samples from simplex distribution with sum constraint\n          - Add local perturbations of parent\n\n      for each candidate in candidate_pool:\n          Use L-BFGS-B minimization minimizing return_c5 (C value):\n              minimize(f=return_c5, x0=candidate, bounds=[(0,1)]*n)\n          After optimization:\n              Repair to ensure [0,1] and integral constraint:\n                  Clip to [0,1]\n                  Scale to meet sum(h)*dx=1.0\n                  Iterate: Clip and scale until integral converges\n\n          Evaluate C\u2085 and update best score if lower\n\n  During optimization:\n      Early termination if: \n          consecutive 10 minimal C\u2085 improvements < 1e-4\n          time_remaining < 90 seconds (stage fallback)\n\n  Return(best_h, best_score, n)\n\n4. Search choices and variations\nKey parameters:\n- Grid doubling sequence: [42, 84, 168, 336, 672]\n- Objective function: return_c5(h) = dx * max(np.correlate(h,1-h,mode=\"full\"))\n- Optimizer: L-BFGS-B with:\n  - Max iterations: 100\n  - Function evaluations: 200\n  - Bounds: Each component [0,1]\n  - Integral enforcement: Projection method:\n      while |sum(h)*dx - 1.0| > 1e-6:\n          h = np.clip(h + scale_factor, 0, 1)\n          scale_factor = -(sum(h)*dx - 1.0)/(sum(h)*dx)\n\nPerturbation strategies:\n- Initial sampling: Use simplex distribution:\n      h0 = np.random.dirichlet(np.ones(n)//scale)  # Initial integral >1.0\n      h0 = h0/scale_factor for scaling adjustment\n- Local search variations:\n      Add Variants for multi-start: Random walk at parent neighborhood\n\nTermination triggers:\n- Time per grid: Decreasing by factor of 0.7 (budget = budget_s * (1/n)**2 )\n- False improvement tracking: Track best C\u2085 update every 10 bFGS iterations\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\nTime estimation: Assuming ~389 function evaluations/sec (grid size penalty)\n  Grid 42: 0.8s budget\n  Grid 84: 3.7s budget\n  Grid 168: ~46s budget (tolerated in 1100-second budget)\n  Higher grids: Similar scaling with integral enforcement penalties\n\nFailure recovery:\n- Hall of Fame: Maintain top 3 solutions per budget\n- Checkpoint: Save best solution before each grid increase\n- Grid fallback: Interpolate best solution from previous grid back to mixed resolution via np.interp\n\nConstraint adherence:\n- Box: Clip in repair phase\n- Integral: Robust projection recalculation\n- Validation: After each score report, enforce normalization and clipping\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n- Final checks:\n  - |sum(h)*dx - 1.0| < 1e-4\n  - All 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1\n  - Return value: (h, final_C, n) \n    where n = number of grid points used\n\n- Re-evaluation: Ensure return_c5 computation exactly matches task metric:\n  dx = 2.0/n\n  corr = np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode=\"full\")\n  final_C = dx * np.max(corr)\n\n- Safety:\n  - Catch DivisionByZero in dx computation\n  - Handle memory limits via grid size cap\n  - Maintain solution diversity with multiple starts per grid\n\nThe plan balances exploitation and exploration by:\n1. Structured grid progression\n2. Each stage uses confined optimization within grid\n3. Repair mechanism preserves feasibility\n4. Early convergence detection",
